<h1>

How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1> <u><b><h4>1. Setup</h4></b></u>Adjust the thigh pad on the lat pulldown machine so your legs are securely locked in place. Reach up and grip the wide bar with an overhand grip (palms facing away), wider 
than shoulder-width.
 
 <u><b><h4>2. Positioning</h4></b></u> Sit down with your feet flat on the floor and lean slightly back at the hips (about 10–15 degrees) while keeping your chest lifted.
 
 <u><b><h4>3. Pulling Phase</h4></b></u> Exhale as you pull the bar down toward your upper chest. Focus on driving your elbows down and back rather than pulling with your arms.
 
 <u><b><h4>4. Peak Squeeze</h4></b></u> Pause briefly at the bottom when the bar reaches upper chest level, squeezing your back muscles together.
 
 <u><b><h4>5. Return Phase</h4></b></u> Inhale as you slowly extend your arms back up under control until your lats feel fully stretched at the top.


<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 
 <b>◉Builds Upper Back Width:</b>Wide grip emphasis targets the outer lats and teres major, creating a V-taper physique.<br>
 
 <b>◉Accessible Lat Work:</b> Provides similar benefits to pull-ups while allowing easy weight adjustment for progressive strength building.<br>
 
 <b>◉Improves Posture:</b> Strengthens upper back pullers, helping counteract rounded shoulder alignment from prolonged sitting.


<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy) | 3 – 4 | 10 – 12 | 60 – 90 seconds |
| Endurance / Definition | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 | 45 – 60 seconds |
| Strength | 4 – 5 | 6 – 8 | 2 – 3 minutes |

Avoid pulling the bar down behind your neck or swinging your torso excessively backward to move heavy weight.

